## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine REST fetch, WebSocket, and Service Worker offline strategies in a practical, real-world scenario.

## Key Concepts
- **DOM updates**: Append-only rendering to avoid duplicate display
- **Fetch**: Initial payload load for historical messages
- **WebSocket**: Real-time message streaming
- **Service Worker**: Offline caching for assets and payload
- **Deduplication**: Using a Set to track seen messageId values

## Code Examples

### REST Fetch
```javascript
async function loadMessages(roomId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/messages?roomId=${roomId}&limit=20`);
  const messages = await res.json();
  messages.forEach(msg => addMessage(msg));
}
```

### WebSocket Handling
```javascript
const ws = new WebSocket(`ws://api.example.com/chat/${roomId}`);
ws.onmessage = (event) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(event.data);
  if (!seenIds.has(msg.id)) {
    seenIds.add(msg.id);
    addMessage(msg);
  }
};
```

### Service Worker Cache Strategy
```javascript
// sw.js
self.addEventListener('fetch', (e) => {
  if (e.request.url.includes('/api/messages')) {
    e.respondWith(
      caches.open('chat-cache').then(cache => 
        cache.match(e.request).then(res => res || fetch(e.request))
      )
    );
  }
});
```

### Minimal Render Function
```javascript
const seenIds = new Set();
function addMessage(msg) {
  if (seenIds.has(msg.id)) return;
  seenIds.add(msg.id);
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.textContent = msg.text;
  document.getElementById('chat').appendChild(el);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates offline-first real-time UI design skills, specifically the ability to handle correct message ordering, deduplication, and robustness to disconnects across fetch, WebSocket, and Service Worker boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket reconnection with exponential backoff and jitter
- Service Worker offline-first fetch caching strategy
- Minimal DOM patching with id-based deduplication
- Offline outgoing message queue with replay on reconnect

## Code Example
```javascript
// fetch wrapper with stale-while-revalidate
async function fetchWithCache(url) {
  const cache = await caches.open('chat-v1');
  const cached = await cache.match(url);
  const response = await fetch(url);
  cache.put(url, response.clone());
  return cached || response;
}

// WebSocket wrapper with exponential backoff
class WSWrapper {
  constructor(url) {
    this.url = url;
    this.queue = [];
    this.reconnect(1000);
  }
  reconnect(delay) {
    this.ws = new WebSocket(this.url);
    this.ws.onclose = () => setTimeout(() => this.reconnect(Math.min(delay * 2, 30000)), delay);
    this.ws.onopen = () => this.queue.forEach(msg => this.ws.send(msg));
  }
  send(msg) { this.ws.readyState === 1 ? this.ws.send(msg) : this.queue.push(msg); }
}

// Patch applier with dedup and ordering
function applyPatch(root, patch) {
  const existing = new Map([...root.querySelectorAll('[data-id]')].map(el => [el.dataset.id, el]));
  patch.sort((a, b) => a.seq - b.seq).forEach(item => {
    if (!existing.has(item.id)) {
      root.appendChild(createMessageEl(item));
    }
  });
}
```

